sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Triangles which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} and {DBC} be triangles on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {AD} and {BC}.

I say that the triangle {ABC} equals the triangle {DBC}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="310" height="210" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.37 diagram" src="propI37.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The proof builds outward from the two triangles: produce AD both ways to
    // E and F, close the two parallelograms, then read each triangle off as
    // half of its own parallelogram. So the walk gates by step — the produced
    // line + drawn parallels arrive on slide 2, the parallelograms on slide 3.
    // Slide 3 drops the TRIANGLE fills so the two parallelograms read cleanly
    // (their fills would otherwise sit on top of the triangles they contain);
    // slides 4-5 bring each triangle back inside its parallelogram, which is
    // exactly the "half of" beat. Hiding a triangle also takes its diagonal
    // (AB / DC) with it — wanted, since the diameters aren't drawn until the
    // slide that names them.
    var BASE = ["A","B","C","D",
                "BC","AD","AC","BD","AB","DC",
                "ABC","BCD"];
    var PROD = BASE.concat(["E","F","EF","BE","CF"]);
    var PARA = ["A","B","C","D","E","F",
                "BC","AD","EF","BE","CF","AC","BD",
                "EBCA","DBCF"];
    var HALF = PROD.concat(["EBCA","DBCF"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.37",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;80,170",
            "C;point;free;170,170",
            "A;point;free;130,40",
            // Invisible carrier: the parallel to BC through A, on which D slides.
            "AD';line;parallel;A,B,C;0;0;0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;AD',170,40",
            // E and F are the fourth vertices of the two parallelograms, and
            // both land on the line AD produced (E-A = B-C, F-D = C-B), which
            // is what makes EF the produced parallel. Both sit on-canvas.
            "E;point;parallelogram;B,C,A",
            "F;point;parallelogram;C,B,D",
            // The base, the original parallel AD, and AD produced to E and F.
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            // The two parallels drawn in the construction: BE ∥ CA, CF ∥ BD.
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            // Parallelogram edges that are also triangle sides — drawn in their
            // own right so they survive slide 3, where the triangles are hidden.
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            // The two diameters, as hover targets only; the triangles draw them.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "DC;line;connect;D,C;0;0;0",
            // Regions. The triangles keep Joyce's black edges and take the
            // colour pair (cyan / pink); the two parallelograms are fill-only
            // (their boundaries are the lines above) and start hidden, since
            // the original figure draws no such regions.
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0,0,0;cyan",
            "BCD;polygon;triangle;B,C,D;0;0;0,0,0;pink",
            "EBCA;polygon;quadrilateral;E,B,C,A;0;0;0;orange",
            "DBCF;polygon;quadrilateral;D,B,C,F;0;0;0;green"
        ],
        aliases: { "DBC": "BCD", "BA": "AB", "CD": "DC", "CA": "AC", "DB": "BD",
                   "EB": "BE", "FC": "CF", "DA": "AD", "FE": "EF" },
        initiallyHidden: ["EBCA","DBCF"],
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABC} and {DBC} be triangles on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {AD} and {BC}. I say that {ABC} equals {DBC}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABC","BCD","BC"] },
            { text: "Produce {AD} in both directions to {E} and {F}. Draw {BE} through {B} parallel to {CA}, and draw {CF} through {C} parallel to {BD}.",
              visible: PROD,
              highlighted: ["EF","BE","CF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "EF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "BE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.2" }, { ref: "I.31" }] },
            { text: "Then each of the figures {EBCA} and {DBCF} is a parallelogram, and they are equal, for they are on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {BC} and {EF}.",
              visible: PARA,
              highlighted: ["EBCA","DBCF","BC"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "EBCA", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "DBCF", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.35" }] },
            { text: "Moreover the triangle {ABC} is half of the parallelogram {EBCA}, for the diameter {AB} bisects it.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["ABC","EBCA","AB"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "ABC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }] },
            { text: "And the triangle {DBC} is half of the parallelogram {DBCF}, for the diameter {DC} bisects it.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["BCD","DBCF","DC"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "BCD", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }] },
            { text: "The halves of equal magnitudes equal one another, therefore the triangle {ABC} equals the triangle {DBC}.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["ABC","BCD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N." }] },
            { text: "So triangles which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["ABC","BCD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.2": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post2/",
        "I.31":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI31/",
        "I.34":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "I.35":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI35/",
        "C.N.":     "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Produce {AD} in both directions to {E} and {F}. Draw {BE} through {B} parallel to {CA}, and draw {CF} through {C} parallel to {BD}. [!just I.Post.2; I.31]

Then each of the figures {EBCA} and {DBCF} is a parallelogram, and they are equal, for they are on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {BC} and {EF}. [!just I.35]

Moreover the triangle {ABC} is half of the parallelogram {EBCA}, for the diameter {AB} bisects it. And the triangle {DBC} is half of the parallelogram {DBCF}, for the diameter {DC} bisects it. [!just I.34]

[!just C.N.]

Therefore the triangle {ABC} equals the triangle {DBC}.

Therefore *triangles which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

In this proposition the triangles have the same base while in the next one the triangles have equal bases. Since the proofs are the same except that this depends on I.35 while the next depends on I.36, and the next is more general, there is no purpose to include this proposition.

The justification of the last conclusion is missing. From the statement that the doubles of two magnitudes are equal, we want to conclude that the magnitudes themselves are equal. Although Euclid included no such common notion, others inserted it later. See the commentary on [Common Notions](../../commonnotions/cn1/) for a proof of this halving principle based on other properties of magnitudes.

#### Use of Proposition 37

This proposition is used in @I.39, @I.41, and @VI.2.
